@page "/legacy"
@* KNOWN ISSUES: this page keeps two intentional defects to demonstrate baselines and regression tests. Unlike the
   showcase examples, its defects are not marked as known: the end-to-end tests audit this page as it is, and the CI gate
   accepts its findings because they are in demo/a11y-baseline.json. *@
<PageTitle>Legacy page · Blazor Accessibility Toolkit</PageTitle>

<div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Sample app page</p>
    <h1>Legacy page</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        A page of an existing app that still uses two components with known defects. This is what adopting the toolkit
        on a real app looks like: you cannot fix everything at once, so the known issues go into a committed baseline and
        only <em>new</em> problems fail the build.
    </p>
</div>

<ul class="reasons">
    <li>The end-to-end test <code>Legacy_page_known_issues_are_still_reported</code> audits this page in a browser and asserts that the unlabelled button is still reported.</li>
    <li>The CI gate passes anyway, because the finding is listed in <code>demo/a11y-baseline.json</code>. Add another unlabelled button here and the gate fails with a <code>[new]</code> finding.</li>
    <li>The clickable card is invisible to the browser audit: axe-core cannot see Blazor's <code>@@onclick</code>. The bUnit test of <code>ClickableCard</code> catches it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The legacy widgets</h2>
<div class="legacy-widgets">
    <UnlabelledIconButton />
    <ClickableCard Title="Old card" Details="Only mouse users can open this card." />
</div>
